 "ai_stories": [
  {
   "id": "lam-supply-chips",
   "headline": "Lam's supply warning hits chip stocks",
   "facts": [
    "Lam CFO: guidance would have been higher without near-term supply constraints; December-quarter slips ship in March quarter",
    "CEO: clean room and component constraints into 2027; 'caught up very shortly'",
    "SOX -6.7% on 22 Oct; Lam about -9%"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/articles/chip-stocks-fall-supply-warning-154508313.html",
    "https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nasdaq-futures-drop-over-1-093849296.html"
   ],
   "paragraph": "Lam Research sells the machines that chipmakers use to build chips, a bit like a company selling ovens to bakeries: if the ovens arrive late, the bread does too. Lam said its revenue guidance would have been a bit higher without near-term supply chain constraints, and its CFO expects shipments slipping out of the December quarter to ship in the March quarter. The CEO said clean room space and certain components will keep limiting chipmakers into calendar 2027, yet also said the company would be \"caught up very shortly\" and that this is \"nothing like\" COVID-era shortages. Those two messages fit awkwardly together. The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index fell 6.7% on Thursday, but Meta spending and oil were also cited, so Lam's role is not proven.",
   "edited": "none",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
   "id": "meta-cash-burn",
   "headline": "Meta's AI spending turns its cash flow negative",
   "facts": [
    "Q3 FCF -$2.1bn, first negative since 2012",
    "Q3 capex $34.9bn vs $19.4bn a year earlier",
    "2026 capex $135bn to $145bn; no 2027 figure",
    "about $825bn commitments (single outlet)",
    "shares about -7%"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://investinglive.com/stocks/meta-shares-fall-7-on-huge-spending-plans/",
    "https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nasdaq-futures-drop-over-1-093849296.html"
   ],
   "paragraph": "Free cash flow is what is left after a company pays for running the business and for building things, like a paycheck minus the mortgage and the renovation bills. Meta's was negative $2.1bn in the third quarter of 2026, its first negative quarter since 2012. The cause is capital spending, or capex, on things like data centres: $34.9bn this quarter against $19.4bn a year earlier. Meta set 2026 capex guidance at $135bn to $145bn and gave no 2027 number. A filing showed about $825bn of future spending commitments, as reported by one outlet. Shares fell about 7%. Does a negative quarter mean trouble? Not by itself. The open question is when the spending earns its cost back.",
   "edited": "reworded commitments clause to 'as reported by one outlet'",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
   "id": "samsung-substrates",
   "headline": "Nvidia and Tesla reportedly fund Samsung chip substrates",
   "facts": [
    "3.7tn won (~$2.8bn): Nvidia 2.6tn, Tesla 1.1tn, per Maeil via Reuters, unconfirmed",
    "FC-BGA substrate lines in Sejong, long-term contract"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/samsung-electromechanics-gets-28-billion-chip-investment-from-tesla-nvidia-report-says-4964838"
   ],
   "paragraph": "An AI chip cannot sit directly on a circuit board. It is mounted on a base layer called an FC-BGA substrate, a bit like an adapter plate that connects the chip to the board. Reuters, citing a South Korean newspaper and unnamed industry sources, reported that Samsung Electro-Mechanics will receive 3.7 trillion won, about $2.8bn, with 2.6 trillion from Nvidia and 1.1 trillion from Tesla. The money would build production lines in Sejong under a long-term supply contract. No company confirmed the amounts. Some reports call this circular financing, which simply means customers funding their own suppliers. Whether that is a worry or sensible planning, and what the final amounts and terms are, remains unknown.",
   "edited": "none",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
   "id": "coreweave-debt",
   "headline": "CoreWeave's debt costs climb",
   "facts": [
    "bonds issued June at 9.675%, ~15% week to 19 Oct",
    "shares about -10% on 22 Oct; HSBC Reduce",
    "Ives estimate 5% to 10% of projects may not proceed"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/coreweave-bond-yields-reach-15-101338344.html",
    "https://www.fxleaders.com/news/2026/10/22/coreweave-pulled-down-10-amid-rising-credit-strain-and-ai-infrastructure-debt/"
   ],
   "paragraph": "CoreWeave rents out AI computing power and has borrowed heavily to build it, a bit like a landlord with a very large mortgage. Its bonds were issued in June 2026 at a 9.675% yield and traded around 15% in the week to 19 October. A higher yield means lenders demand more for the same loan because they see more risk of not being repaid. Shares fell about 10% on Thursday, and HSBC repeated a \"Reduce\" rating. One analyst estimated 5% to 10% of planned AI infrastructure projects may not proceed, but that is an estimate, not a confirmed figure. Deutsche Bank's Jim Reid said AI financing costs \"aren't dramatic overall\". Whether this is an overreaction or the start of tighter financing is unknown.",
   "edited": "completed the final sentence's grammar",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  }
 ],
